chx:Document a doc:ReportSection ; rdfs:label "Second-Layer Applications"@en ; skos:definition "This document renews chapter 14 of the 3rd edition of Mastering Bitcoin for SEN0401 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14/research> ;
    doc:hasSection chx:S_PlatformGuarantees, chx:S_ColoredCoins, chx:S_PaymentChannels, chx:S_LightningNetwork, chx:S_SinceThePrint .
chx:S_PlatformGuarantees a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Platform guarantees"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Platform guarantees" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 1 ;
    skos:definition "The chapter starts from the view that Bitcoin is a platform: a list of guarantees such as no double spend, immutability, accounting and non-expiration can be combined into applications, and the book names the notary and the crowdfunding transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Here each guarantee was tested on a private chain of Bitcoin Core 31.1, so that the claim is a result and not only a sentence. This subject covers Chain guarantees; Composed applications." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#PlatformGuarantees> .
chx:S_ChainGuarantees a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Chain guarantees"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Chain guarantees" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 2 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PlatformGuarantees ;
    skos:definition "The guarantees of the base system that the chapter lists are the bricks of every application, and four of them were run: a second spend of an output is refused, outputs cannot exceed inputs, a signed transaction does not expire, and the list itself has 18 entries (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This subject covers A double spend is rejected; Outputs cannot exceed inputs; A transaction does not expire; Eighteen building blocks." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ChainGuarantees> .
chx:S_DoubleSpendRejected a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A double spend is rejected"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A double spend is rejected" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 3 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChainGuarantees ;
    skos:definition "The first guarantee on the chapter's list is that no output can be spent twice in the same valid chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). We tested it on a private chain: the first payment of an output paid a fee of 100,000 satoshis and entered the memory pool; a second payment of the same output with a lower fee of 50,000 was refused as an insufficient replacement; a second payment with the higher fee of 200,000 replaced the first, which Bitcoin Core now allows by default without any signal (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); and after a block had confirmed the winner, an attempt to send the first transaction again was refused because its input was already spent. The memory pool is only a waiting room, so what the guarantee really says is that the chain of blocks will contain at most one of the two, and nothing is final before the block. It is like two people holding tickets for the same seat: the usher lets only one of them in, and which one is decided by who is processed first, not by who arrived first." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#DoubleSpendRejected> .
chx:S_OutputsCannotExceedInputs a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Outputs cannot exceed inputs"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Outputs cannot exceed inputs" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 4 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChainGuarantees ;
    skos:definition "The accounting guarantee says that the outputs of a transaction cannot exceed its inputs, so that value cannot be created in a payment (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). We built a transaction whose outputs asked for more than the output it spent and the node refused it with the reason bad-txns-in-belowout, the very reason that appeared when one pledger of the crowdfunding test below sent his part alone and the sum fell short of the goal. The rule is applied to every transaction by every node, and a miner who ignores it produces a block that the others reject, which is why the ledger can be trusted without a bookkeeper. It is like a cashier who counts the notes in the drawer before and after each sale: the books balance every time, and a mistake of one coin is found at once." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#OutputsCannotExceedInputs> .
chx:S_NonExpiration a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A transaction does not expire"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A transaction does not expire" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 5 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChainGuarantees ;
    skos:definition "A valid transaction does not expire: if it is valid today it will be valid later, as long as its inputs remain unspent and the rules do not change (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the test chain we signed a payment at block height 301 and kept it back; at height 451, 150 blocks later, the node still accepted it. This property is what lets the parties of a payment channel keep signed transactions in a drawer for weeks, and it is also why a channel needs special rules to cancel an old state, because an old signed transaction never goes bad by itself (Poon and Dryja, 2016). It is like a cheque without an expiry date: it can be cashed years later, so the only way to make it useless is to empty the account it is drawn on." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#NonExpiration> .
chx:S_EighteenBuildingBlocks a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Eighteen building blocks"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Eighteen building blocks" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 6 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChainGuarantees ;
    skos:definition "The chapter begins with a list of guarantees, which it calls building blocks, from no double spend, immutability and neutrality to timelocks, forgery protection and predictable issuance (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Counted in the saved text of the chapter the list has 18 entries, and the chapter says plainly that it is not complete and that new blocks are added with each new feature, as the package rules and the pay-to-anchor output of Bitcoin Core 28.0 show (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024). The practical lesson is that an application should name the guarantees it relies on, because each one has conditions, for example the depth of confirmation for immutability or the unchanged rules for non-expiration. It is like the list of materials in a building plan: the builder can only promise a roof if the walls are certified to carry it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#EighteenBuildingBlocks> .
chx:S_ComposedApplications a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Composed applications"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Composed applications" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 7 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PlatformGuarantees ;
    skos:definition "Two applications of the chapter combine several guarantees: a proof of existence needs immutability and a timestamp, and a Lighthouse-style crowdfunding transaction needs integrity and atomicity, and both were built on the test chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The first public purchase of goods for bitcoin, the pizzas of Laszlo Hanyecz in May 2010, is the old story behind the idea that bitcoin can be used for trade (Hanyecz, 2010). This subject covers Proof of existence; Lighthouse crowdfunding." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ComposedApplications> .
chx:S_ProofOfExistence a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Proof of existence"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Proof of existence" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 8 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ComposedApplications ;
    skos:definition "A digital notary needs nothing but a commitment recorded in a transaction: the data stays with its owner and only its fingerprint goes into the chain, where immutability and the timestamp of the block do the rest (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the test chain we computed the 32-byte SHA-256 of a document, wrote it as an OP_RETURN output with a value of zero, had the node confirm it, and read the output script back as OP_RETURN followed by exactly that hash; from then on the document is proved to have existed no later than that block. Bitcoin Core relays such data-carrier outputs by default up to a size that its help text states (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026). It is like posting a sealed copy of your manuscript to yourself: the postmark proves the date, the seal proves the content, and nobody else has to read it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ProofOfExistence> .
chx:S_LighthouseCrowdfund a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Lighthouse crowdfunding"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Lighthouse crowdfunding" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 9 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ComposedApplications ;
    skos:definition "The Lighthouse style of crowdfunding combines three guarantees, integrity, atomicity and consistency: each pledger signs his own input and the single agreed output with the ANYONECANPAY flag, and the transaction cannot be spent until the goal is funded (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the test two pledges of 1.0005 and 1 coin met a goal of 2 coins plus a fee of 50,000 satoshis: with one pledge alone the node refused the transaction because the outputs exceeded the inputs, with both it accepted it, and changing the output after the signatures made the script check fail, because the signature hash of the signed input covers the output (Lau and Wuille, 2016). The flag that makes this possible is explained in the signature chapter of the book (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is like a petition sheet that is only valid when the last signature has filled it: the earlier names stay on the sheet, but the office does not act on it before." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#LighthouseCrowdfund> .
chx:S_ColoredCoins a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Colored coins"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Colored coins" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 10 ;
    skos:definition "Colored coins are the oldest application of the chapter: marking bitcoin to stand for other assets, in the beginning by writing data into transactions (Rosenfeld, 2012), and since the proposals of single-use seals by committing off the chain to contract terms that only the parties see (Todd, 2017). The chapter's three mechanisms, seals, pay to contract and client-side validation, are rebuilt here by hand on the test chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This subject covers Seals and contracts; Asset protocols." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ColoredCoins> .
chx:S_SealsAndContracts a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Seals and contracts"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Seals and contracts" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 11 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ColoredCoins ;
    skos:definition "The three mechanisms of modern colored coins are an output that can be spent once, a public key that commits to a contract, and a receiver who checks the history, and each was built by hand and tested against the node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Wuille et al., 2020). This subject covers An output is a single-use seal; The pay to contract tweak; The contract stays off the chain; Client-side validation walk." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#SealsAndContracts> .
chx:S_UtxoIsASingleUseSeal a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "An output is a single-use seal"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "An output is a single-use seal" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 12 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SealsAndContracts ;
    skos:definition "A single-use seal is a thing that can be closed over a message only once, like the tamper-evident seal on a parcel, and an unspent transaction output is exactly that, because it can be spent once in a valid chain and the spending transaction is the message (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Peter Todd described this idea for asset transfer in a text dated 5 December 2017 (Todd, 2017). In the test, three outputs served as seals in a chain of transfers, each earlier one was spent by the transaction that carried the next commitment, and the three outpoints were all different, so each seal was closed once. It is like a numbered sheet of postage stamps that the post office cancels when used: the number can appear on only one cancelled letter." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#UtxoIsASingleUseSeal> .
chx:S_PayToContractTweak a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The pay to contract tweak"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The pay to contract tweak" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 13 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SealsAndContracts ;
    skos:definition "Pay to contract lets two parties agree on contract terms and then tweak the receiver's public key with a hash of those terms, so that the output commits to the contract while looking like any other key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). We recomputed it in Python: the tweaked key equalled the plain key plus the tweak times the generator point, the output was not the same as the plain pay-to-witness-key-hash output, the revealed key and contract were accepted by the check while a wrong contract was rejected, and the node accepted a spend with the tweaked key and refused one with the plain key, with the script failure at the hash comparison. The same construction became part of the basis of taproot, whose rules are in BIP 341 (Wuille et al., 2020). It is like writing the terms of a deal inside the ink of a signature: nobody can see them, but whoever knows them can show that the signature carries them." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#PayToContractTweak> .
chx:S_ContractStaysOffChain a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The contract stays off the chain"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The contract stays off the chain" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 14 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SealsAndContracts ;
    skos:definition "Because only the tweaked key is published, the contract can be as long and detailed as the parties like, in any language, and nothing is revealed about it, not even that it exists (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the test the contract was 69 bytes, the outputs on the chain were ordinary pay-to-witness-key-hash outputs, and the token amounts were not visible anywhere in the transactions. The Optech topic on client-side validation describes the same trade: the data lives with the owners and the chain only anchors it (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like a letter in a closed envelope: the post office sees the address and the stamp, and the content is between sender and receiver." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ContractStaysOffChain> .
chx:S_ClientSideValidationWalk a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Client-side validation walk"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Client-side validation walk" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 15 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SealsAndContracts ;
    skos:definition "Since the nodes do not check the contract, the receiver does, and this is client-side validation: the sender hands over the history of the asset and the receiver checks that every step is anchored in a transaction and follows the contract rules (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the test the history had three steps, an issue of 100 tokens and two transfers; the checker recomputed each commitment against the public key in the output on the chain and found all three equal, and a forged contract text did not match. The receiver needs only the history that concerns his coins, which is also the privacy gain the book names, and which Optech lists among the properties of the protocols that use the method (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like buying a used car with its service book: the buyer checks every stamp against the garage, and does not need the records of other cars." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ClientSideValidationWalk> .
chx:S_AssetProtocols a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Asset protocols"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Asset protocols" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 16 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ColoredCoins ;
    skos:definition "RGB and Taproot Assets are the two protocols that combine the three mechanisms and that can ride on Lightning channels, and their sources are the first colored-coin overview of 2012 and the single-use-seal text of 2017 (Rosenfeld, 2012); (Todd, 2017); (RGB developers, 2026). This subject covers The history of colored coins; RGB and Taproot Assets." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#AssetProtocols> .
chx:S_ColoredCoinsHistory a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The history of colored coins"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The history of colored coins" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 17 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_AssetProtocols ;
    skos:definition "Colored coins are the oldest of the applications in the chapter: Meni Rosenfeld's overview is dated 4 December 2012 and describes marking small amounts of bitcoin to stand for other assets (Rosenfeld, 2012), the Bitcoin Wiki still describes the term as a class of methods for real-world assets (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026), and Peter Todd's single-use-seal design that the book builds on is dated 5 December 2017, five years later (Todd, 2017). The book names the first implementation Enhanced Padded-Order-Based Coloring and then three newer mechanisms, seals, pay to contract and client-side validation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The shift in those five years was from writing the asset data into the chain to keeping it off the chain and anchoring it only by commitments. It is like moving from stamping the message on the banknote itself to filing it in a private ledger that points at the banknote's number." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ColoredCoinsHistory> .
chx:S_RgbAndTaprootAssets a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "RGB and Taproot Assets"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "RGB and Taproot Assets" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 18 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_AssetProtocols ;
    skos:definition "The two protocols that the chapter names for colored coins are RGB and Taproot Assets, and both are built on seals, pay to contract and client-side validation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The RGB site describes its smart contracts as post-blockchain and says that nothing is kept on the chain (RGB developers, 2026); the documentation of Lightning Labs describes Taproot Assets with Lightning in mind (Lightning Labs, 2026); and the Optech topic lists RGB, Taro and Taproot Assets together as client-side validation protocols (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). The book adds the note that taproot is part of Bitcoin and Taproot Assets is not, and that forwarding an asset over Lightning can be native or translated, with the risk of a free option for the receiver in the translated case. We did not install either protocol; they are in the list of what was not executed. It is like two currencies exchanged at a booth that is in the city but not run by the city: the booth uses the city's bank for settlement and keeps its own books." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#RgbAndTaprootAssets> .
chx:S_PaymentChannels a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Payment channels"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Payment channels" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 19 ;
    skos:definition "A payment channel moves many payments between two parties while the chain sees two transactions, the funding and the settlement, and the book builds it up in three steps: the one-way channel with refund timelocks, the asymmetric revocable commitment, and the hash time lock contract (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The first public designs of such channels are described by the Bitcoin Wiki and by Decker and Wattenhofer (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Decker and Wattenhofer, 2015). This subject covers The one-way channel; Revocable commitments; Hash time lock contracts." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#PaymentChannels> .
chx:S_OneWayChannel a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "The one-way channel"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The one-way channel" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 20 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaymentChannels ;
    skos:definition "The one-way channel of Emma and Fabian shows the funding output, the signed commitments and the refund with a lock time, and the numbers of the book were reproduced and the contracts tested on the private chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Decker and Wattenhofer, 2015). This subject covers The arithmetic of Emma and Fabian; Funding needs both keys; The refund timelock ladder." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#OneWayChannel> .
chx:S_EmmaFabianArithmetic a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The arithmetic of Emma and Fabian"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The arithmetic of Emma and Fabian" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 21 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OneWayChannel ;
    skos:definition "The streaming-video example of the book is a channel in which Emma pays Fabian 0.01 millibit for each second, which is 0.00001 bitcoin or 1,000 satoshis, so an hour costs 3,600,000 satoshis, the 36 millibits that she locks, and 600 seconds cost 600,000 and leave 3,000,000 for her refund (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same sums were used on the test chain: the funding output held 3,600,000 satoshis and the settlement after six seconds paid Fabian 6,000 satoshis. The idea that a stream of tiny payments can be settled by one transaction at the end is an old one, discussed under micropayments long before Lightning (Wikipedia contributors, 2026), and the Bitcoin Wiki records the channel techniques that followed (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). It is like a taxi meter: the fare grows every second in the cab, and only the final amount is paid at the door." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#EmmaFabianArithmetic> .
chx:S_FundingNeedsBothKeys a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Funding needs both keys"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Funding needs both keys" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 22 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OneWayChannel ;
    skos:definition "The funding transaction pays into a 2-of-2 multisignature output, so that neither party can take the money without the other (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The witness script of the test channel was 71 bytes long, starting with the opcode for 2 and ending with 2 and OP_CHECKMULTISIG; the funding transaction was 165 virtual bytes; a spend signed by Emma alone was refused by the node with the script error about the signature check, and a spend signed by both was accepted. This is also the weakness the book points out: after funding, Emma needs Fabian's signature even to get her money back, which is why the refund must be signed before the funding is broadcast (Decker and Wattenhofer, 2015). It is like a safe-deposit box that needs two keys: the bank cannot open it alone and neither can you, which is secure until one key holder disappears." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#FundingNeedsBothKeys> .
chx:S_RefundTimelockLadder a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The refund timelock ladder"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The refund timelock ladder" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 23 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OneWayChannel ;
    skos:definition "The book solves the vanishing-partner problem with transaction-level lock times: Emma obtains a signed refund with a lock time 30 days ahead, which is 4,320 blocks at 144 a day, and every later commitment gets a lock time one block shorter, so that the newest can be broadcast first (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the test chain six commitments had lock times from 488 down to 483; at the start all six were refused as non-final, at height 483 only the sixth was accepted, and once it was mined the earlier ones were refused with missing inputs, because the sixth had spent the shared funding output. The book then shows why this is not good enough: it fixes the life of the channel and limits the number of updates to the number of blocks, which is why revocation was invented (Poon and Dryja, 2016). It is like a row of postdated cheques in which the latest has the earliest date: the bank honours the first it can, and the others bounce." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#RefundTimelockLadder> .
chx:S_RevocableCommitments a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Revocable commitments"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Revocable commitments" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 24 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaymentChannels ;
    skos:definition "The asymmetric revocable commitment replaces the ladder of lock times by a penalty: an output delayed by a relative lock time and a revocation key of which each party holds half, which together make cheating costly, as the book's exchanges Hitesh and Irene show (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Friedenbach et al., 2015). This subject covers The asymmetric commitment; Revocation needs both halves; The penalty takes everything; The 48-bit state index." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#RevocableCommitments> .
chx:S_AsymmetricCommitment a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The asymmetric commitment"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The asymmetric commitment" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 25 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RevocableCommitments ;
    skos:definition "In the asymmetric scheme each party holds a commitment transaction that pays the other side at once and pays himself only after a delay, so that broadcasting costs the broadcaster time (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The delayed output has the script of the book: IF the revocation key, ELSE 1000 blocks, CHECKSEQUENCEVERIFY, DROP and the owner's key, then CHECKSIG. On the test chain a spend of that output with 999 confirmations was refused as non-BIP68-final, with 1000 it was accepted, and a spend whose input carried no sequence number was refused by the script with the lock time not satisfied; the rules are in BIP 68 and BIP 112 (Friedenbach et al., 2015); (BtcDrak et al., 2015). The commitment was 181 virtual bytes. It is like a loan whose payout date is written on the cheque: the bank pays the other party now but makes you wait." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#AsymmetricCommitment> .
chx:S_RevocationNeedsBothHalves a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Revocation needs both halves"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Revocation needs both halves" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 26 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RevocableCommitments ;
    skos:definition "The revocation key is built so that both parties know the public key but each knows only half of the private key, and each reveals his half when he moves on to a new state (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the test chain a spend of the penalty branch signed with one half was refused by the node with the signature check failing, a spend signed with the sum of both halves was accepted and confirmed, and its witness had three items of 72, 1 and 77 bytes in the recorded run: the signature, whose length varies between 71 and 72 bytes from run to run, a one that selects the revocation branch, and the script. The derivation that lnd uses is specified in BOLT 3 and checked below against the published vectors (Lightning developers, 2026). It is like a vault door with a dial each partner sets: once you have told your half of the number to the other, he alone can open it if you ever try to cheat." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#RevocationNeedsBothHalves> .
chx:S_PenaltyTakesEverything a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The penalty takes everything"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The penalty takes everything" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 27 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RevocableCommitments ;
    skos:definition "The punishment is what makes revocation work, and the book gives the numbers: the channel of 10 coins moves from 5 and 5 to 7 for Hitesh and 3 for Irene, going back to the old state would give Irene 2 more, but if she broadcasts it Hitesh has the revocation key and takes all 10 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Irene would lose her whole 3 and Hitesh would gain 3 over his due of 7, so cheating has a negative expected value as soon as there is any chance of being caught. On the hand-built channel the penalty spend was confirmed, and on the lnd nodes the same thing happened with a justice transaction, described below. The catch, which the book states, is that the wronged party must watch the chain and answer within the delay, which is the reason for watchtowers (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like a deposit forfeited for breaking a lease: the landlord need not prove a loss, he simply keeps all of it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#PenaltyTakesEverything> .
chx:S_StateIndexBits a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The 48-bit state index"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The 48-bit state index" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 28 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RevocableCommitments ;
    skos:definition "The book says that the state of a Lightning channel is identified by a 48-bit index, which allows 281 trillion changes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The exact figure is 2 to the power 48, 281,474,976,710,656, or about 2.8 times 10 to the 14. BOLT 3 derives the per-commitment secret of each state from one seed with a chain of hashes that flips bits of the seed, and its test vectors include the secret of the highest index for a seed of all ff bytes, which our Python code reproduced as 7cc854b5 and so on (Lightning developers, 2026). The shachain lets the other side store all revealed secrets in a small tree instead of a long list. It is like the numbering of lottery tickets from a single seed: you can print the ticket for any number, but you cannot guess the next one from the last." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#StateIndexBits> .
chx:S_HashTimeLockContracts a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Hash time lock contracts"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hash time lock contracts" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 29 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaymentChannels ;
    skos:definition "The hash time lock contract turns two parties' channel into a link of a chain: a secret opens it, a time closes it. The idea was discussed as atomic transfers between chains years before Lightning (Nolan, 2013) and is explained for the book in the Bitcoin Wiki (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); here its two branches and its hash were tested on the node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This subject covers The HTLC secret branch; The HTLC timeout branch; RIPEMD-160 of SHA-256." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#HashTimeLockContracts> .
chx:S_HtlcSecretBranch a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The HTLC secret branch"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The HTLC secret branch" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 30 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_HashTimeLockContracts ;
    skos:definition "A hash time lock contract commits funds to a secret: whoever shows the preimage R of the hash H, together with the receiver's signature, can take the payment (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the test chain the hand-built contract paid out with the right 32-byte secret and the receiver's key, refused a wrong secret at the hash comparison, refused the right secret with the payer's key, and, in the book's basic form without a key in the first branch, paid anyone who knew the secret. The Bitcoin Wiki and Optech describe the construction and its uses in channels and atomic swaps (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like a locker whose code is known to one person: the code opens it, and the code alone is enough." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#HtlcSecretBranch> .
chx:S_HtlcTimeoutBranch a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The HTLC timeout branch"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The HTLC timeout branch" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 31 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_HashTimeLockContracts ;
    skos:definition "The second branch gives the money back if the secret is never shown: the payer can claim a refund after the absolute time in a CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY, so that nobody's funds stay locked forever (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the test chain the contract was funded at height 1495 with a lock at 1514, 19 blocks later; the refund was refused as non-final before the lock and one block short of it, accepted at the lock, and a refund transaction whose own lock time was lower than the one in the script was refused by the script, as BIP 65 requires (Todd, 2014). In a routed payment the lock times shrink along the path, so each node can still refund himself after the next one has failed. It is like a gift card with an expiry: if nobody claims it before the date, it goes back to the buyer." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#HtlcTimeoutBranch> .
chx:S_HashIsRipemdOfSha a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "RIPEMD-160 of SHA-256"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "RIPEMD-160 of SHA-256" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 32 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_HashTimeLockContracts ;
    skos:definition "There is a small difference between the book's script and the real one: the book writes HASH160 of H, and BOLT 3 writes RIPEMD160 of the SHA-256 of the preimage in the scripts for the 20-byte hash comparison (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Lightning developers, 2026). On the test chain the hand-built contract used a 20-byte hash, equal to the RIPEMD-160 of the 32-byte payment hash, and the witness script of the book's form came to 101 bytes. The payment hash in an invoice is the 32-byte SHA-256 of the secret, so one secret serves every hop. It is like two stamp sizes for one seal: the big one on the invoice, the small one inside the lock, both made from the same die." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#HashIsRipemdOfSha> .
chx:S_LightningNetwork a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "The Lightning Network"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The Lightning Network" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 33 ;
    skos:definition "The Lightning Network links channels into a routed network, first described by Joseph Poon and Thaddeus Dryja (Poon and Dryja, 2016) and specified today in the BOLT documents (Lightning developers, 2026). The chapter shows a payment from Alice to Eric through three intermediaries; here the same payment was made on five lnd nodes, and the cryptography of the transport, the onion and the invoice was recomputed from the published test vectors. This subject covers The routed payment; Transport and onion; The channel on the chain." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#LightningNetwork> .
chx:S_RoutedPayment a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "The routed payment"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The routed payment" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 34 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_LightningNetwork ;
    skos:definition "The routed payment of the book was run on five lnd nodes with the book's fees, and its amounts, timeouts and balances, the hold invoice and the invoice itself are compared with the book's steps (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Lightning developers, 2026). This subject covers The fee arithmetic; The timelock staircase; Balances after the payment; A hold invoice in flight; The structure of an invoice." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#RoutedPayment> .
chx:S_FeeArithmetic a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The fee arithmetic"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The fee arithmetic" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 35 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RoutedPayment ;
    skos:definition "The routed payment of the book is a staircase of amounts: to deliver 1 bitcoin through three intermediaries who each want 0.001, Alice must offer Bob 1.003, Bob offers Carol 1.002, Carol offers Diana 1.001, and Diana pays Eric 1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). We built that network of five lnd nodes on a private chain, with channels of 4 bitcoin and the three middle nodes charging a base fee of 0.001 and no proportional fee; the payment of 1 bitcoin cost exactly 300,000 satoshis in fees, and the hop amounts reported by the route were 100,200,000, 100,100,000 and 100,000,000 satoshis, the book's staircase scaled to satoshis. Each fee rewards the node for tying up its balance until the secret comes back, and the fee fields are part of the instructions in the onion (Lightning developers, 2026). It is like a relay of couriers each adding a tip to the envelope: the last one gets the parcel, and every earlier one keeps his tip." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#FeeArithmetic> .
chx:S_TimelockStaircase a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The timelock staircase"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The timelock staircase" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 36 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RoutedPayment ;
    skos:definition "Every hop of a routed payment must have more time than the next, so that a node that has paid forward can still collect from the one behind it: the book's HTLCs expire in 10, 9, 8 and 7 blocks, a step of one block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the real network of five lnd nodes the expiries of the hops stepped down by 80 blocks twice and by 0 at the last hop, the invoice asked for a final delta of 80, and the sample configuration of lnd 0.21.3 gives the same 80 as the default timelock delta (Lightning Labs, 2026). A step of one block would be unsafe, because a congested chain can delay a transaction by more than that, so the real number is a margin for the time a node needs to get its refund or claim on to the chain (Lightning developers, 2026). It is like the cut-off times of a chain of banks: each bank closes its window earlier than the one it relies on, with enough hours left to react." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#TimelockStaircase> .
chx:S_BalancesAfterPayment a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Balances after the payment"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Balances after the payment" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 37 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RoutedPayment ;
    skos:definition "At the end of the book's example Alice has 0.997 and Bob 3.003 in their channel, each intermediary has earned 0.001, and Eric has received 1 bitcoin (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The nodes of the test network ended in the same way: Alice's balance fell by 100,300,000 satoshis, each of Bob, Carol and Diana gained 100,000, Eric gained 100,000,000, and the sum of all changes was exactly zero, so nothing was created or lost on the way; Alice's side of her channel stood at 99,696,530 and Bob's at 300,300,000, the 0.997 and 3.003 of the book once the small commitment fee of the channel is counted. No transaction touched the chain in the process, and the commitment fee is only deducted from the funder's side of each channel (Lightning developers, 2026). It is like moving money between the tills of a row of shops by notes in the shared ledger: the cash in the drawers does not move until someone closes the shop." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#BalancesAfterPayment> .
chx:S_HoldInvoiceInFlight a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A hold invoice in flight"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A hold invoice in flight" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 38 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RoutedPayment ;
    skos:definition "A hold invoice lets the receiver delay the release of the secret, which makes the HTLCs of the book's steps 2 to 5 visible at the same time on all hops (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the test network a hold invoice for 0.1 bitcoin was paid by Alice: while the receiver withheld the secret, every one of the five nodes showed an HTLC pending in a channel; when the receiver settled the invoice, the secret travelled back and the payment succeeded; a second hold invoice was cancelled, the HTLCs failed back along the route, and the balances of all channels were exactly what they had been before. Optech describes the contract that makes this possible and its use also in atomic swaps (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like a parcel held at a depot: everybody on the route has signed for it, and it either goes through when the recipient signs or goes back to the sender." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#HoldInvoiceInFlight> .
chx:S_InvoiceStructure a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The structure of an invoice"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The structure of an invoice" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 39 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RoutedPayment ;
    skos:definition "The invoice is how the receiver gives the sender the hash H: a bech32 string, the format of BIP 173, that BOLT 11 fills with the network prefix, the amount, a timestamp and tagged fields for the payment hash, the payment secret, the expiry and the final delay (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017); (Lightning developers, 2026). The invoice of the test payment had the prefix lnbcrt1, which our decoder read as the regtest network and 1 bitcoin, which is 100,000,000,000 millisatoshi; the payment hash, the payment secret and the expiry in its fields were the same as the ones lnd reported, the final delta was 80, and the secret that came back after payment hashed to the payment hash with SHA-256. That last check is the proof of payment: the sender holds a secret that only the receiver could have released. It is like a receipt that is also a key: whoever holds the matching stub can prove the shop took the money." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#InvoiceStructure> .
chx:S_TransportAndOnion a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Transport and onion"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transport and onion" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 40 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_LightningNetwork ;
    skos:definition "The privacy of Lightning rests on an encrypted transport and an onion of layered messages, and both were recomputed in Python from the published test vectors of BOLT 8, BOLT 4 and BOLT 11 (Lightning developers, 2026); (Danezis and Goldberg, 2009). This subject covers The Noise handshake vector; The transport message vector; The onion packet vector; The fixed size of the onion; Onion integrity by HMAC; The invoice vector." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#TransportAndOnion> .
chx:S_NoiseHandshakeVector a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The Noise handshake vector"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The Noise handshake vector" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 41 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransportAndOnion ;
    skos:definition "All traffic between Lightning nodes is encrypted and authenticated point to point; the book states it in one sentence (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and BOLT 8 specifies the handshake as Noise_XK with secp256k1, ChaCha20-Poly1305 and SHA-256 in three acts (Lightning developers, 2026). The Noise Protocol Framework is the same family of designs that other secure channels use (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). We wrote the initiator in pure Python and fed it the fixed keys of the published test vector: it produced the 50-byte first act and the 66-byte third act of the vector and the sending key beginning 969ab31b, all equal to the published values. Because the initiator already knows the responder's static key, an eavesdropper learns neither side's identity from the first acts. It is like a secret handshake at a club door in which each side proves a piece of knowledge before showing a face." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#NoiseHandshakeVector> .
chx:S_TransportMessageVector a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The transport message vector"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The transport message vector" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 42 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransportAndOnion ;
    skos:definition "After the handshake every message is sent as an encrypted two-byte length with a 16-byte tag and then the encrypted body with another 16-byte tag, and the keys rotate after 1,000 uses (Lightning developers, 2026). The vector sends the five bytes hello: our code, using the sending key from the handshake, produced the 39 bytes that BOLT 8 lists, which are 2 plus 16 plus 5 plus 16, and the ciphertext equalled the published one. The overhead of 34 bytes per message is the price of hiding even the length of a message from a listener on the network. It is like sending every letter in a sealed bag with a second sealed bag for its length: the courier cannot see how long the letter is." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#TransportMessageVector> .
chx:S_OnionPacketVector a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The onion packet vector"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The onion packet vector" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 43 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransportAndOnion ;
    skos:definition "The onion that Alice builds follows the Sphinx design, in which each hop peels one layer and learns only the next hop (Danezis and Goldberg, 2009); the book describes it in prose (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). BOLT 4 gives a test vector of five hops with the session key 41 repeated, the five node keys, the payloads and the associated data 42 repeated: our Python code derived the shared secrets, the filler and the layers and produced the packet of 1366 bytes that equals the vector byte for byte (Lightning developers, 2026). The test also shows the variable-length payload: the last hop of the vector carries 275 bytes while the others carry fewer than 90, and the packet size does not change. It is like a Russian doll of letters in which every doll is sealed with a different stamp: the first postman can open only the outer one." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#OnionPacketVector> .
chx:S_OnionFixedSize a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The fixed size of the onion"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The fixed size of the onion" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 44 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransportAndOnion ;
    skos:definition "A node must not learn from the size of the packet how far it is from the destination, so the packet has a fixed size whatever the length of the path (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Built with one, two or five hops from the vector's keys and payloads, the packet was always 1366 bytes: one version byte, a 33-byte public key, 1300 bytes of hop payloads and a 32-byte HMAC, as BOLT 4 defines (Lightning developers, 2026). The unused room is filled with a pseudo-random stream derived from the session key, and a filler is added as each layer is peeled so that the packet keeps its size and its integrity check (Danezis and Goldberg, 2009). It is like a fixed-size envelope used for every letter: a thick and a thin message arrive in the same envelope." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#OnionFixedSize> .
chx:S_OnionIntegrityByHmac a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Onion integrity by HMAC"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Onion integrity by HMAC" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 45 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransportAndOnion ;
    skos:definition "Each hop checks the integrity of the packet before it does anything: it derives a shared secret from its own private key and the ephemeral key in the packet, takes the key mu from it and compares the HMAC of the routing bytes and the associated data with the HMAC in the packet (Lightning developers, 2026). With the private key of the first node from the vector, the vector packet passed, and the same packet with one bit flipped, at the first bit or in the very last bit of the 1300 routing bytes, failed. The book's promise that intermediate nodes can verify and decrypt their portion of the route information rests on this check (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is like a tamper-evident seal on every layer: one scratch on the wrapper and the courier refuses the parcel." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#OnionIntegrityByHmac> .
chx:S_InvoiceVector a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The invoice vector"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The invoice vector" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 46 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransportAndOnion ;
    skos:definition "BOLT 11 lists invoices to test a parser, among them the one for a cup of coffee, 2500 micro-bitcoin, to be paid within a minute (Lightning developers, 2026). Our decoder read the prefix lnbc2500u as the main network and 2500 micro-bitcoin, which is 250,000,000 millisatoshi or 250,000 satoshis, the timestamp as 2017-06-01 10:57:38 in UTC, the payment hash as 32 bytes, the expiry as 60 seconds and the signature as 65 bytes. The format uses the bech32 alphabet and checksum of BIP 173 but without its 90-character limit (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). It is like reading a price tag in a foreign unit: the prefix tells the unit, the digits the quantity, and the tail the proof that the shop wrote it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#InvoiceVector> .
chx:S_ChannelOnChain a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "The channel on the chain"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The channel on the chain" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 47 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_LightningNetwork ;
    skos:definition "What a channel looks like on the chain was read from real transactions of the test network: the funding output, a commitment with anchors, a cooperative close and a justice transaction after a breach, with the public network's size taken from a statistics page for comparison (Lightning developers, 2026); (Mempool Space, 2026). This subject covers The funding output is a multisignature; The shape of a commitment transaction; The revocation key vector; A cooperative close is one transaction; The justice transaction; The size of the public graph." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ChannelOnChain> .
chx:S_FundingOutputIsMultisig a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The funding output is a multisignature"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The funding output is a multisignature" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 48 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChannelOnChain ;
    skos:definition "The funding transaction of a channel pays into one output that both nodes must sign to spend (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In lnd the output is a pay-to-witness-script-hash of a 2-of-2 multisignature script; on the test network its value was 4 bitcoin, its type witness_v0_scripthash, and the script read as 2, two 33-byte keys, 2 and OP_CHECKMULTISIG with the keys in lexicographic order, as BOLT 3 requires (Lightning developers, 2026). The funding transaction was 165 virtual bytes. Optech notes that newer lnd versions can use taproot with MuSig2 for the funding output, which makes the channel look like any other payment (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like a safe-deposit box whose two keys are always filed in the same order: anyone can check that the box is the right one without opening it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#FundingOutputIsMultisig> .
chx:S_CommitmentTransactionShape a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The shape of a commitment transaction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The shape of a commitment transaction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 49 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChannelOnChain ;
    skos:definition "Every commitment transaction of an lnd channel carries the marks of BOLT 3: the high bytes of the lock time and the sequence are fixed at 0x20 and 0x80, and the lower 24 bits of each hold half of the obscured 48-bit commitment number, so that a watcher cannot read the state number from it (Lightning developers, 2026). The revoked commitment that Irene's node broadcast in the test was a version-2 transaction of 279 virtual bytes with four outputs: two anchors of 330 satoshis each, one for each side, the delayed output of the owner, and the immediate output of the other side. The anchors let either side raise the fee of a stuck commitment later, which is why the fee does not have to be guessed in advance (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like a form with a fixed header: the clerk can see it is a valid form, but the case number is written in a code that only the parties can read." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#CommitmentTransactionShape> .
chx:S_RevocationKeyVector a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The revocation key vector"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The revocation key vector" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 50 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChannelOnChain ;
    skos:definition "The revocation key of the book is built from two secrets, and BOLT 3 gives the exact formulas: the key is the revocation base point times the hash of the base point and the per-commitment point, plus the per-commitment point times the hash in the other order (Lightning developers, 2026); the private key is derived with the same weights. From the base secret and the per-commitment secret of the appendix our Python code derived the local key by adding the hash times the generator to the base point, the revocation public key and the revocation private key, and all three equalled the published values, as did the base point. The point of the formula is that neither the base secret alone nor the per-commitment secret alone can sign, which is the book's 'each party knows only half' (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is like a door that opens only when two numbers are dialled together: each partner holds one number, and neither can open the door alone." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#RevocationKeyVector> .
chx:S_CooperativeCloseIsOneTx a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A cooperative close is one transaction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A cooperative close is one transaction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 51 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChannelOnChain ;
    skos:definition "The channel of the book lives on two transactions of the chain, the funding and the settlement, and anything in between stays off the chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the test network Bob and Carol closed their channel by agreement: the closing transaction had a lock time of 0 and final sequence numbers, so it was valid at once, spent the funding output with a witness of an empty item, two signatures and the script, had two taproot outputs worth 399,995,175 satoshis in total, and was 193 virtual bytes. The 4,825 satoshis missing from the 4 bitcoin are the fee. A cooperative close avoids the delays of a unilateral one, which BOLT 5 describes for the cases when the other side does not answer (Lightning developers, 2026). It is like closing a joint account at the counter with both signatures: the money is yours the same day, and nobody has to wait out a notice period." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#CooperativeCloseIsOneTx> .
chx:S_BreachJusticeTransaction a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The justice transaction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The justice transaction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 52 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChannelOnChain ;
    skos:definition "The book's Hitesh and Irene example was run on two lnd nodes with a channel of 10 bitcoin: they moved 2 bitcoin so that the state went from 5 and 5 to 7 for Hitesh and 3 for Irene (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). We then stopped both, restored Irene's directory from a copy made at the first state, and had her force-close, which published the revoked commitment; Hitesh, restarted, saw the breach and published a justice transaction with two inputs, his own immediate output with a witness of 72 and 37 bytes, and Irene's delayed output through the revocation branch with the items 72, 1 and 77 (the signature is 71 or 72 bytes from run to run), and his wallet received 999,990,380 satoshis, 9,620 less than the whole 10 bitcoin, the difference being the fee. The node reported the channel as closed by a breach, and Hitesh took 3 bitcoin more than his due of 7, exactly the book's penalty (Lightning developers, 2026). It is like a bank that, finding you used a cancelled cheque, closes the account and gives the whole balance to the other holder." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#BreachJusticeTransaction> .
chx:S_MainnetGraphSize a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The size of the public graph"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The size of the public graph" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 53 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChannelOnChain ;
    skos:definition "The book calls the Lightning Network proposed and shows five nodes; the statistics page of mempool.space, fetched on 15 October 2026, reported for its latest day, 30 August 2026, 32,518 channels, 16,225 nodes and a total capacity of 375,370,668,018 satoshis, which is about 3,753.7 bitcoin (Mempool Space, 2026). That makes an average of 4.0 channels per node, and the same page gives the tip of the Bitcoin chain at the time of fetching as height 971,604 (Mempool Space, 2026). The figures are a snapshot of a public service that can change by the hour and are not part of the executed experiments; the Wikipedia article says that Lightning Labs launched the network in 2018 (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). It is like the number of branches of a bank printed on its brochure: it tells the scale, not the experience of any one customer." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#MainnetGraphSize> .
chx:S_SinceThePrint a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Since the print"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Since the print" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 54 ;
    skos:definition "Between the print of the book and October 2026 the Lightning Network went into operation, Bitcoin Core gained package relay, version-3 transactions and pay to anchor, replacement became the default, and several details of the chapter turned out to differ from the real software (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Mempool Space, 2026). This subject lists these changes, a candidate erratum in the source file, and the limits of what the sandbox could run. This subject covers Errata and drift; New machinery; Limits of the experiments." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#SinceThePrint> .
chx:S_ErrataAndDrift a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Errata and drift"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Errata and drift" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 55 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SinceThePrint ;
    skos:definition "Between the print and today several details of the chapter have drifted: the anchor in the source file, the word proposed for a network in operation, the date of the paper, the delay and the fees of real channels (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Poon and Dryja, 2016). This subject covers The chapter anchor erratum; Lightning is no longer proposed; The date of the whitepaper; A delay of 1,000 versus 2016; The book fee versus the network fee." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ErrataAndDrift> .
chx:S_ChapterAnchorErratum a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The chapter anchor erratum"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The chapter anchor erratum" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 56 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ErrataAndDrift ;
    skos:definition "The AsciiDoc source of chapter 14 begins with the anchor [[ch12]], although the chapter is the fourteenth of the book and the file of chapter 12 begins with its own anchor [[mining]] (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The anchor is the label that cross-references point to, so a reference written for chapter 12 would land in chapter 14; the same kind of left-over was found in the file of chapter 13, which begins with [[ch11]]. The errata appendix of the book concerns the Bitcoin whitepaper, not the book, and does not mention the anchor, so this is a candidate for a correction to report to the authors and not a known erratum (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is like a street sign left from the old numbering after the houses were renumbered: the houses are fine, but a visitor following the sign arrives at the wrong door." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ChapterAnchorErratum> .
chx:S_LightningNoLongerProposed a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Lightning is no longer proposed"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Lightning is no longer proposed" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 57 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ErrataAndDrift ;
    skos:definition "The chapter introduces the Lightning Network as a proposed routed network of bidirectional payment channels, which was true of a design in 2015 and is no longer true of a network in operation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Wikipedia says that Lightning Labs launched the network in 2018 (Wikipedia contributors, 2026), and the statistics page of mempool.space counted 32,518 channels and 16,225 nodes on 30 August 2026 (Mempool Space, 2026); the glossary of the book still calls it a proposed implementation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The word survives from the first edition's time, and a reader should read it as a historical remark. It is like a bridge that a guidebook describes as planned: today people drive over it every day, and the guidebook is out of date." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#LightningNoLongerProposed> .
chx:S_WhitepaperDate a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The date of the whitepaper"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The date of the whitepaper" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 58 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ErrataAndDrift ;
    skos:definition "The book dates the first description of the network to February 2015 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and Wikipedia agrees, citing a copy of version 0.5 of the paper archived on 28 February 2015 (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). The paper that is kept at lightning.network today is the draft version 0.5.9.2 dated 14 January 2016, 320 days after that archive date (Poon and Dryja, 2016), and the same site keeps a summary of the idea as a short slide document (Poon and Dryja, 2015). So the book is right about the first version and the file one reads today is a later revision of the same draft, with the same two authors, Joseph Poon and Thaddeus Dryja. It is like a first-edition date on a book of which you hold the fourth printing: the date is right, and the pages are not the same." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#WhitepaperDate> .
chx:S_CsvDelayThousandVersus2016 a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A delay of 1,000 versus 2016"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A delay of 1,000 versus 2016" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 59 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ErrataAndDrift ;
    skos:definition "The book's example uses a revocable delay of 1,000 blocks, and the hand-built channel of the test enforced exactly that: 999 confirmations were refused and 1000 accepted (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The lnd channels on the test network reported a delay of 2016 blocks, two weeks at ten minutes a block, and a channel reserve of one percent of the capacity, 4,000,000 satoshis of a 400,000,000-satoshi channel; the delay is a parameter that the two nodes negotiate in the open message of BOLT 2 as to_self_delay and that BOLT 3 uses in the script (Lightning developers, 2026). A longer delay gives the wronged party more time to react to a breach, at the price of a longer wait for an honest unilateral close. It is like the notice period in a lease: a longer one protects the party who must react, and delays the one who wants to leave." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#CsvDelayThousandVersus2016> .
chx:S_BookFeeVersusNetworkFee a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The book fee versus the network fee"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The book fee versus the network fee" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 60 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ErrataAndDrift ;
    skos:definition "The book's fee of 0.001 for each of three hops is 0.3 percent of the payment, a teaching number chosen to make the arithmetic visible (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The statistics page of the public network gave a median base fee of 500 millisatoshi and a median proportional fee of 100 parts per million on 30 August 2026, and with those medians a payment of 1 bitcoin over three hops would cost about 30,001 satoshis, 0.03 percent, ten times less than in the book (Mempool Space, 2026). The same page gives averages that are much higher, 904 millisatoshi and 823 parts per million, so a few expensive nodes pull the mean up and the median is the better guide to what a sender can find. It is like the price of a taxi ride in a city: the posted tariff is high, but most rides cost far less." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#BookFeeVersusNetworkFee> .
chx:S_NewMachinery a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "New machinery"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "New machinery" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 61 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SinceThePrint ;
    skos:definition "New machinery in Bitcoin Core and in the Lightning ecosystem since the print is covered here: package relay and anchors, default replacement, proposals that are still drafts, the other implementations and the features of lnd (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Harding and Todd, 2015); (Zhao, 2024). This subject covers TRUC and anchors; Full RBF is standard; The proposals are still drafts; Other implementations; The lnd defaults." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#NewMachinery> .
chx:S_TrucAndAnchors a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "TRUC and anchors"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "TRUC and anchors" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 62 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_NewMachinery ;
    skos:definition "A channel party who must publish a commitment in a time of high fees needs a way to add fee afterwards, and Bitcoin Core 28.0 gave it one: version-3 or TRUC transactions with topology limits, package relay for a child with its parents, and a standard pay-to-anchor output that anyone can spend (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Zhao, 2024); (Sanders, 2025). On the test chain a version-3 parent of 123 virtual bytes with a zero-value anchor output and a fee of zero was refused alone for its minimum relay fee, and accepted together with a child of 151 virtual bytes through the submitpackage call, with an effective rate of about 10.9 satoshis per virtual byte; the anchor was spent with an empty witness, and both transactions were then confirmed (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Optech describes the ephemeral-anchor idea behind it (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like a car that cannot start by itself and is towed by another: neither would pass the fee test alone, but the pair travels at the average speed." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#TrucAndAnchors> .
chx:S_FullRbfIsStandard a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Full RBF is standard"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Full RBF is standard" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 63 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_NewMachinery ;
    skos:definition "BIP 125 defines replacement that is allowed only for a transaction that signalled its opt-in (Harding and Todd, 2015). Bitcoin Core 28.0 changed the default of the mempoolfullrbf option from 0 to 1 (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024), and the help of Bitcoin Core 31.1 no longer lists the option at all (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026): replacement is simply the rule. On the test chain the second payment of the same output with a higher fee replaced the first in the memory pool, whatever the first had signalled. For channels this means that a counterparty can try to replace a commitment, and it is one reason for the topology rules and anchors above. It is like an auction in which a higher bid always replaces the earlier one, without the earlier bidder having agreed to it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#FullRbfIsStandard> .
chx:S_ProposalsStillDrafts a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The proposals are still drafts"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The proposals are still drafts" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 64 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_NewMachinery ;
    skos:definition "Several improvements that Lightning designers want are not in Bitcoin: the signature type of BIP 118 for eltoo-style updates, the template opcode of BIP 119, and the point time locked contracts that need schnorr adaptor signatures (Decker and Towns, 2017); (Rubin, 2020); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). The saved BIP files of 118, 119, 431 and 433 all carry the status Draft, and Optech calls eltoo a proposed enforcement mechanism for the Lightning Network that would let a later state replace an earlier one without penalties. A reader should therefore treat any text that relies on them, including text about channel factories and PTLCs, as a plan, not as a feature. It is like a house with a drawing for an extension: the drawing is public and detailed, but one cannot yet sleep in the new room." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#ProposalsStillDrafts> .
chx:S_OtherImplementations a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Other implementations"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Other implementations" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 65 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_NewMachinery ;
    skos:definition "The book says that at least five open source teams have implemented the Lightning Network and coordinate through the BOLT standards (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The sources saved here name Core Lightning, a standard-compliant implementation in C (Core Lightning developers, 2026), Eclair, the Scala implementation of ACINQ (ACINQ, 2026), the Lightning Development Kit in Rust (Spiral, 2026), and lnd, the Go implementation that was the only one installed and run, in version 0.21.3-beta (Lightning Labs, 2026). The BOLTs are maintained as an open repository whose readme says the specifications are in progress (Lightning developers, 2026). The interoperability between implementations was not tested here. It is like several car makers building to the same road rules: each model drives on the same road, but we only drove one." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#OtherImplementations> .
chx:S_LndDefaults a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The lnd defaults"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The lnd defaults" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 66 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_NewMachinery ;
    skos:definition "A recent lnd node advertises features that the chapter does not know: the node of the test reported 17 feature names, among them anchor commitments with zero-fee HTLC transactions and gossip queries, which are listed in the feature table of BOLT 9 (Lightning developers, 2026). Its sample configuration gives the defaults of the node, a base fee of 1000 millisatoshi, a timelock delta of 80 blocks and a minimum channel size of 20,000 satoshis (Lightning Labs, 2026), and its release notes record the changes of the 0.21 line, including fixes for taproot channels (Lightning Labs, 2026). Simple taproot channels use MuSig2 in the funding output, which Optech describes (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like the options list of a modern car against the one printed in an old manual: the engine is the same idea and the dashboard has grown." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#LndDefaults> .
chx:S_Limits a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Limits of the experiments"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Limits of the experiments" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 67 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SinceThePrint ;
    skos:definition "A research record is only as good as its honesty about what was not done, and the ten items that the sandbox could not run are listed with their reasons (Lightning developers, 2026). This subject covers What was not executed." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#Limits> .
chx:S_WhatWasNotExecuted a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "What was not executed"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "What was not executed" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 68 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Limits ;
    skos:definition "Not everything in the chapter could be run in the sandbox, and the record lists ten items with their reasons: the public network, the other implementations, Taproot Assets and RGB software, a watchtower, an HTLC expiring on the chain, multipath and trampoline routing and offers, gossip on the public network, the transport between lnd nodes on the wire, the signature of the lnd release manifest, and a unilateral close with HTLCs pending. Each of them is either replaced by a hand-built or recomputed equivalent that is named in the leaves above, or marked as read from a source only (Lightning developers, 2026). The honest reading of the results is therefore: the mechanisms of the chapter work as described on a private chain, the numbers of the public network are a snapshot, and the behaviour under the load and the attacks of the real network was not tested; Optech describes channel jamming as an example of what a private test cannot show (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like a test drive on a closed track: it proves that the car runs, and not how it behaves in traffic." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#WhatWasNotExecuted> .
